narrative:
## One Type, Many Possibilities

A **generic type** is a type that has a type parameter — a placeholder that gets filled in when you use it.

### Defining a Generic Box

```xiom
type Box[T] = {
  value: T;
}

fn Box.get_value[T](self) -> T {
  return self.value;
}

fn Box.set_value[T](self: &mut Box[T], new_value: T) {
  self.value = new_value;
}
```

The `[T]` after `Box` is the **type parameter**. When you create a `Box[Int]`, `T` becomes `Int`. When you create `Box[Str]`, `T` becomes `Str`.

### Using Box with Different Types

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  var int_box: Box[Int] = Box[Int]{ value: 42 };
  io.println(int_box.get_value());
  int_box.set_value(99);
  io.println(int_box.get_value());

  let str_box: Box[Str] = Box[Str]{ value: "Hello" };
  io.println(str_box.get_value());

  let bool_box: Box[Bool] = Box[Bool]{ value: true };
  io.println(bool_box.get_value());

  return 0;
}
```

### Why Not Separate Types?

Without generics, you'd write:

```xiom
type IntBox = { value: Int; }
type StrBox = { value: Str; }
type BoolBox = { value: Bool; }
```

Triple the code, triple the maintenance. With generics, `Box[T]` handles all three — and any future type you dream up.

### Type Inference

When you write `Box[Int]{ value: 42 }`, XIOM knows `T = Int` from the type annotation. You must specify the type at creation — there's no guessing.

